This is a complete, step-by-step breakdown of your project's runtime execution flow based precisely on the actual code in your workspace.

PART 1 — RUNNING THE CLIENT
When you open a terminal and run npm run dev in the client folder, here is the exact execution chain:

Terminal command ↓ package.json script FILE: client/package.json The dev script is executed: "vite". ↓ Build/development tool The vite executable starts the Vite development server. ↓ Configuration Vite automatically loads vite.config.ts. ↓ Port & Server Vite starts an HTTP server on port 5173 (default). The URL http://localhost:5173/ becomes available. ↓ HTML FILE: client/index.html When the browser requests the URL, Vite serves this file. ↓ JavaScript/TypeScript entry point FILE: client/src/main.tsx The HTML file contains <script type="module" src="/src/main.tsx"></script>, telling the browser to load it. ↓ Root component FILE: client/src/App.tsx main.tsx imports the <App /> component and mounts it to the <div id="root"></div>. ↓ Router Inside App.tsx, the <BrowserRouter> component initializes the routing system. ↓ Page/component The router looks at the URL and renders the matching component (e.g., <LoginPage /> for /). ↓ Browser The browser paints the HTML output to the screen.

PART 2 — RUNNING THE SERVER
When you open another terminal and run npm run dev in the server folder:

Terminal command ↓ package.json script FILE: server/package.json The dev script is executed: "tsx watch src/index.ts". ↓ Server entry point The tsx engine runs FILE: server/src/index.ts. ↓ Application initialization FILE: server/src/app.ts index.ts imports app from app.ts, where const app = express(); initializes the Express application. ↓ Configuration & Environment index.ts loads dotenv/config to read the .env file and imports config from src/config to set the port and environment. ↓ Middleware Inside app.ts, global middleware is registered: helmet() for security, cors() for cross-origin requests from the client, rateLimit() to prevent spam, express.json() to parse request bodies, and morgan() for logging. ↓ Routes Inside app.ts, route prefixes are bound to their route files. For example: app.use('/api/voting', votingRoutes);. ↓ Database connection Inside index.ts, prisma.$connect(); is called to establish the connection to MySQL. ↓ Server listening Inside index.ts, app.listen(config.port, ...) binds the server to the configured port (e.g., 5000), making the API ready to receive requests.

