PART 5 — COMPARE THE TWO URLS
http://localhost:5173/ Router Path: / ↓ Page: LoginPage ↓ Hooks: useState, useForm, useNavigate ↓ API Calls: None on load. ↓ Server endpoints: None on load.

http://localhost:5173/voting-machine Router Path: /voting-machine ↓ Page: VotingMachinePage ↓ Hooks: useState, useCallback ↓ API Calls: None on load. ↓ Server endpoints: None on load.

Why do they show different screens? Because client/src/App.tsx acts like a switchboard. When the browser URL changes, the React Router <Routes> component looks for the matching <Route> tag and instructs React to only execute the component function defined in the element prop.

PART 6 — IMPORTANT CONCEPT: "DOES THE FILE RUN?"
In a modern React application, "running a file" means several different things depending on the exact stage of the lifecycle:

File Loaded: The browser downloads the .tsx file via HTTP from the Vite server. (e.g., both App.tsx and LoginPage.tsx are downloaded).
Module Evaluated: The JavaScript engine reads the file from top to bottom once. Code outside of component functions runs immediately. (e.g., const loginSchema = z.object(...) in LoginPage.tsx is evaluated exactly once).
Component Function Executed: React literally calls your component like a standard function: LoginPage(). This happens every time state changes.
Component Rendered: React takes the JSX returned by the component function and transforms it into real HTML DOM elements on the screen.
useEffect Executed: Code inside useEffect(() => { ... }) runs after the component has rendered on the screen. (e.g., useEffect(() => { start(); }, []) in OTPScreen runs only after the OTP screen is visible).
Event Handler Executed: Code inside onClick or onSubmit does not execute when the file loads or renders. It is saved in memory and only executes when the user physically clicks or types.
Executed after API: Code inside a .then() or await block pauses execution and only resumes after the server sends a response.
